DECISION 2026-05-12 (Mark, interactive): rich error discrimination, no baked-in degradation policy. Bridge exposes structured BridgeError + PerCertError; callers choose hard-fail vs lenient vs graceful-degrade. Framework-not-policy answer.

BRIDGE-LEVEL ERRORS (fail the whole call):
- BinaryNotFound { path: PathBuf }
- BinaryNotExecutable { path: PathBuf, detail: String }
- SubprocessFailed { exit_code: Option<i32>, stderr: String }
- SubprocessKilled { signal: i32 }
- SubprocessTimeout { elapsed_secs: u64 }
- OutputParseError { detail: String }

All variants Clone + Debug + PartialEq + Eq + Send + Sync + non_exhaustive; serde behind feature flag per non-negotiable #6.

PER-CERT ERRORS (live inside batch results; don't fail the whole batch):
- MalformedDer { detail: String }
- UnsupportedCertType { detail: String }
- Other { detail: String }

Specific variants of PerCertError are implementation-detail; will be discriminated based on actual error shapes zlint emits.

BATCH RESULT SHAPE: Result<Vec<Result<Vec<(CheckId, Verdict)>, PerCertError>>, BridgeError>

VERSION-MISMATCH: not checked. Runtime enumeration (per .jy95.1 = A2) means there's no version expectation to mismatch. Output-format incompatibility surfaces as OutputParseError naturally.

TIMEOUT: bridge constructor accepts BridgeConfig with default timeout (initial value 60s per batch invocation; configurable). Generous default; consumers tighten if needed.

CALLER PATTERNS:
- Strict: `let bridge = ZlintBridge::new()?;` propagates any setup error
- Lenient: `let bridge_opt = ZlintBridge::new().ok();` None means skip
- Hybrid: pattern-match on BridgeError variants for fine-grained handling

CASCADE: PKIX-jy95.7 (pkix-zlint-bridge) and PKIX-jy95.8 (pkix-pkilint-bridge) both implement this BridgeError + PerCertError pattern. Specific PerCertError variants for each bridge may differ (zlint vs pkilint error vocabularies) but the structural pattern is symmetric.
